; a horizontal structural surface inside a built environment.
; the indoor equivalent of the ground.
; supports everything resting on it via normal force.
; collision surface for falling objects inside rooms, buildings.
; made of various materials: wood, stone, concrete, tile — each with its
; own coefficient of restitution with the falling object.
; 
; the floor is kshetra: the bounded field in which indoor physics happens.
; things roll on floors, slide on floors, bounce on floors.
; friction with floor: rolling objects decelerate. sliding objects decelerate.
; 
; the floor IS the mathematical floor function made spatial.
; math floor(x) = greatest integer ≤ x — the lower bound that cannot be passed.
; physical floor = the spatial lower bound that cannot be passed.
; the abheda: both are the same structure — a lower limit that truncates downward.
; the ball's height cannot go below the floor. x cannot go below floor(x).
; same principle: physical space and number space share the same boundary grammar.
; math abheda: the floor operation is this structure in number space

(kosha floor
  (vishesa structures-varga common-sense-varga)
  (swarupa horizontal indoor)
  (yukta normal-force collision-varga friction-force domain-3d)
  (sthita gravity)
  (abheda floor))
